# Deploying the paymaster to a testnet.
#
#   cp contracts/.env.example contracts/.env      # .env is gitignored; .env.example is not
#   $EDITOR contracts/.env
#   set -a && source contracts/.env && set +a
#   forge script script/DeployPaymaster.s.sol:DeployPaymaster \
#     --rpc-url "$RPC_URL" --broadcast --verify --private-key "$DEPLOYER_KEY"
#
# For more than one chain use ./deploy/deploy-chains.sh instead, which reads deploy/chains.json,
# preflights every chain before broadcasting anything, and generates the backend's CHAINS from the
# broadcast receipts rather than from anything typed by hand.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT YOU NEED BEFORE YOU START
# ================================================================================================
#
#   1. An RPC URL for the testnet. A public one works for a first deploy; a provider endpoint
#      (Alchemy, Infura, QuickNode) is worth having, because `--verify` and the deploy's own
#      preflight make a lot of calls.
#
#   2. A funded deployer account. Base Sepolia and OP Sepolia need Sepolia ETH bridged; Sepolia
#      itself needs a faucet. Budget roughly:
#        ~0.01 ETH   contract deployment gas
#         DEPOSIT_WEI  (default 1 ETH — lower it on a testnet, see below)
#         STAKE_WEI    (default 0.021 ETH — a testnet-sized number that a STOCK bundler rejects;
#                       see the note below on the minimum your bundler enforces)
#
#   3. The sponsorship signer's ADDRESS. Not its key: the contract only ever stores the address,
#      and the key lives in the backend (or, in production, in a KMS the backend calls).
#
#   4. An owner address. The deploy hands ownership over at the end via Ownable2Step, and the
#      handover is NOT complete until the new owner calls `acceptOwnership()`.
#
#   5. An Etherscan-family API key, if you want the source verified. Optional — the deploy
#      succeeds without it and ./deploy/verify-contracts.sh can verify later.
#
#   6. The EntryPoint must already exist on the chain. It does on every mainstream testnet, at the
#      canonical address below. If it does not, the deploy fails before broadcasting rather than
#      producing a paymaster that reverts AA34 on every operation.
#
# ================================================================================================


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chain
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base Sepolia (84532). Others: Sepolia 11155111, OP Sepolia 11155420, Arbitrum Sepolia 421614.
#
# THIS ONE MUST RUN A CUSTOM JS TRACER. It is the deploy's RPC, and it is also the endpoint the
# bundler traces against — rundler's safe mode validates with `debug_traceCall` and a JavaScript
# tracer, and most providers do not serve that:
#
#   works:        QuickNode, Chainstack (enable "Debug and Trace APIs" — off by default),
#                 self-hosted geth/erigon/reth with --http.api debug
#   does NOT:     Alchemy and Infura (built-in tracers only; they answer
#                 -32600 "invalid tracer value"), every free public Sepolia endpoint
#                 (-32601, no debug_* at all)
#
# Check before you commit to a provider — this takes 30 seconds and has saved two wrong turns:
#   ./deploy/check-rpc.sh https://your-endpoint
RPC_URL=https://sepolia.base.org

# The bundler's OTHER lane, and the reason a free tier is enough.
#
# Only `debug_*` needs the tracing provider above. Everything else — measured at 369 ordinary reads
# per sponsored operation against 3 trace calls — is plain `eth_call`/`eth_getLogs` that any
# endpoint serves. deploy/rpc-router splits them by method, so a tracing provider's tight
# per-second cap is spent only on the calls that genuinely require it.
#
# Without this split, QuickNode's free 15/second cap is exhausted by traffic that has nothing to do
# with validating, and every operation dies with "internal error: rpc provider error".
#
# Optional: unset, the router falls back to a public endpoint. Alchemy's free tier is a good fit
# here precisely because the thing it cannot do is never asked of it.
ALCHEMY_RPC_URL=

# EntryPoint v0.7. The same address on every chain that has one, because it is deployed through a
# deterministic deployer. Leave it unless you are on a chain that did it differently — the deploy
# checks the address has code and refuses to continue if it does not.
ENTRYPOINT=0x0000000071727De22E5E9d8BAf0edAc6f37da032


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Who deploys, who owns, who signs
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The account that broadcasts. It owns the paymaster FOR THE DURATION OF THE DEPLOY, because
# `addStake` is owner-only — the script stakes first and hands ownership over last. A previous
# version of it did the reverse and left a funded, unstaked paymaster that no bundler would accept.
#
# On a testnet a hot key is fine. On mainnet prefer --account (a cast wallet keystore) or --ledger,
# and leave this unset.
DEPLOYER_KEY=

# Who ends up owning the paymaster: pause, signer rotation, stake withdrawal, and — on
# TenantPaymaster — nominating who may withdraw a tenant's balance.
#
# Use a MULTISIG on mainnet. Whatever you put here must be able to call `acceptOwnership()`, or the
# contract is stuck with the deployer as owner.
PAYMASTER_OWNER=

# The sponsorship signer's ADDRESS. The backend signs attestations with the matching key; the
# contract recovers this address and refuses anything else.
#
# The single most common deploy mistake is a mismatched pair here — the failure is an on-chain
# AA34 on every sponsorship, which points at the signature and not at this line.
PAYMASTER_SIGNER=


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which contract
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# "verifying" — one operator, one shared deposit. The right choice for a self-hosted paymaster
#               sponsoring your own dApp.
# "tenant"    — a balance per customer inside one staked contract, for the multi-tenant SaaS shape.
#               Costs an extra `postOp` on every operation, which a single-tenant deployment should
#               not pay for.
#
# The backend must agree: set `paymasterKind` on the chain's entry in CHAINS to the same value, or
# every sponsorship on that chain fails to validate. The two contracts use different EIP-712
# domains and different `paymasterAndData` layouts, so their signatures are not interchangeable.
PAYMASTER_KIND=verifying


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Money
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The deposit pays for sponsored gas. At zero, every sponsored operation fails AA31.
# 0.05 ETH — plenty for a testnet, and easy to top up with `cast send <paymaster> "deposit()"`.
DEPOSIT_WEI=50000000000000000

# The stake is what makes a bundler ACCEPT this paymaster at all. Both contracts read their own
# storage during validation, which ERC-7562 only permits for a staked paymaster — so an unstaked
# deploy is not "less safe", it is non-functional.
#
# 0.021 ETH here is a testnet convenience, and it is also the deploy script's default. It is
# BELOW rundler's stock 1 ETH minimum on purpose: on a testnet the stake comes out of a faucet.
#
# The consequence is not optional. A bundler you run must be started with
#   --min_stake_value 21000000000000000
# or it rejects every operation with -32502 before anything reaches the chain — nothing on chain
# to show for it, and the error points at the bundler rather than at this line. A stock or public
# bundler will reject this stake outright. For production, raise this to the bundler's minimum.
STAKE_WEI=21000000000000000

# How long between requesting an unstake and being able to withdraw. One day. This is a floor a
# bundler may also enforce a minimum on; it is also how long your capital is locked when you leave.
UNSTAKE_DELAY_SEC=86400


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Verification (optional)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Etherscan v2 covers most chains with one key. Without it, drop --verify and run
# ./deploy/verify-contracts.sh later; an explorer being down is not a reason to fail a deploy that
# already landed on chain.
ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=


# ================================================================================================
# AFTER THE DEPLOY
# ================================================================================================
#
#   1. Accept ownership from PAYMASTER_OWNER. Until this happens the deployer still owns it:
#        cast send <paymaster> "acceptOwnership()" --rpc-url "$RPC_URL" --private-key <owner-key>
#
#   2. Point the backend at it. Add the chain to CHAINS in backend/.env — `paymaster` is the
#      deployed address and `paymasterKind` MUST match PAYMASTER_KIND above:
#
#        CHAINS=[{"chainId":84532,"name":"Base Sepolia","rpcUrls":["https://sepolia.base.org"],
#          "entryPoint":"0x0000000071727De22E5E9d8BAf0edAc6f37da032",
#          "paymaster":"0x...","paymasterKind":"verifying",
#          "explorerUrl":"https://sepolia.basescan.org",
#          "nativeCurrency":{"symbol":"ETH","decimals":18},
#          "minDepositWei":"10000000000000000","minStakeWei":"100000000000000000","enabled":true}]
#
#   3. Set SPONSORSHIP_SIGNER_KEY in backend/.env to the key whose address is PAYMASTER_SIGNER.
#      The backend refuses to start with both that and SPONSORSHIP_SIGNER_KMS_KEY_ID set.
#
#   4. Check it end to end before trusting it. The backend validates CHAINS at boot — it will
#      refuse a chain whose EntryPoint has no code — and `/health/ready` reports per-chain state.
#
#   5. Watch the deposit. `FundingMonitor` alerts below `minDepositWei`, and a deposit that hits
#      zero fails every operation on that chain with no other warning.
